7. **The carriers that read the table** — no code beyond the table for the socket's `call` (`host.rs` runs
   `Session::run`), the command line (`cli.rs` builds its usage line and argv from `VERBS`), MCP (`mcp/listing.rs`
   lists `VERBS`; `mcp/result.rs` answers one text block), `describe.rs` and the recorder (`recording.rs` keeps any
   `Level::Instance` row). Move every count pin to fourteen verbs, ten instance verbs and thirteen field kinds:
   - `schema_tests.rs` (:19 order, :28, :52, the shape rows ~:229);
   - `command.rs:898`;
   - `describe.rs:145` (`[14, 14, 12, 3, 8, 4, 15]`);
   - `mcp_tests.rs:408` and the listing row walk through its `#[track_caller]` helpers;
   - `cli_tests.rs` (an `actions` row beside `graph`'s);
   - `recording_tests.rs` (a kept `actions` step with its answer whole);
   - `mcp.rs:58` and `lib.rs:31-32` docs.

   Keeps tests S4.

13. **The statement and the front page** — `docs/capabilities.md`:
    - the verb as a code span, «fourteen verbs», and its claim under `## What it can do`;
    - its carried set under `## What an MCP client carries onward` (ids, owner ids, verb names, cause words, booleans);
    - its stated limits under `## Stated limits`: accepted is not acting, `press` and `advance` not per node, an
      unpaged answer, its Cells size;
    - its check under `## Where each claim is proven`.

    `README.md:49`: «the fourteen driver commands as tools». `.github/scripts/test_capability_statement.py` needs no
    edit where its `NUMBER_WORDS` reaches twenty (it does, :22-26). The secret and host-path searches over the three
    files stay at 0.

- **(matrix#v030-06) met.** On the six served screens, both modes, `stand_actions` holds:
  - `each_served_screen_lists_its_actionable_nodes_with_the_tables_verbs`: the listed set, its verbs and counts;
  - `every_listed_cause_on_the_lean_tasks_is_what_the_call_returns`: every pair on the four lean tasks equal to
    `Session::run`, and 3 `disabled` controls;
  - `the_stated_cells_and_load_page_causes_are_what_the_call_returns`: `cells-grid`, `cell-A0`, the last column-A
    cell in view and the one past it, and `load-fetch` with its fetch in flight;
  - `the_flight_bookers_options_read_covered_until_the_list_opens`: the two options `covered` with owner
    `flight-type`, `flight-return-date` `disabled`;
  - `the_last_crud_row_reads_off_screen_until_a_scroll`: `crud-person-14` `off-screen` until a `scroll` and the fade.
  - The matrix reads `implemented` with ref
    `tests/blitz-tests/tests/stand_actions.rs::every_listed_cause_on_the_lean_tasks_is_what_the_call_returns`.

- **Tests:** unit tests in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` at the end of the source file (apps/browser/src/about_pages.rs:; apps/browser/src/browser_history.rs:; apps/browser/src/favicon.rs:; apps/browser/src/url_suggestions.rs:; apps/browser/persistence/src/lib.rs:; packages/blitz-dom/src/net.rs:; packages/blitz-dom/src/mutator.rs:; packages/blitz-dom/src/document.rs:; packages/blitz-dom/src/stylo_to_parley.rs:; packages/blitz-dom/src/util.rs:; packages/blitz-dom/src/node/scrollbar.rs:; packages/blitz-dom/src/node/element.rs:; packages/blitz-dom/src/node/node.rs:; wpt/runner/src/test_runners/fuzzy.rs:; wpt/runner/src/test_runners/js_wrapper.rs:; wpt/runner/src/test_runners/harness_test.rs:; wpt/runner/src/test_runners/mod.rs:); eight unit-test modules sit in files of their own, each a `#[cfg(test)]` child declared through `#[path]` at its parent — dioxus-native-dom's bridge tests, declared at the end of `dioxus_document.rs` as `#[cfg(test)] #[path = "dioxus_document_tests.rs"] mod bridge_tests;`, a child of the module under test so they reach its private fields with no constructor, `pub` or `cfg(test)` shim added (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/dioxus_document.rs:), and, since 2026-10-16-file-splits, blitz-dom's `document::changed_set_tests` and `document::font_reading_tests` in `document_changed_set_tests.rs` and `document_font_reading_tests.rs` (packages/blitz-dom/src/document.rs:; packages/blitz-dom/src/document.rs:) and escher-driver's `mcp::tests` and `schema::tests` in `mcp_tests.rs` and `schema_tests.rs` (packages/escher-driver/src/mcp.rs:; packages/escher-driver/src/schema.rs:), each moved out whole with its test paths unchanged;
 each integration test file covers one behavior and opens with a `//!` doc naming the behavior and any guarded bug (tests/blitz-tests/tests/anonymous_block_leak.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/comment_layout.rs:); regression tests cite upstream issue or PR URLs (tests/blitz-tests/tests/animations.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/render_blocking_stylesheet.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/stale_interaction_state.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/stale_node_mapping.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/custom_widget_layout.rs:); documents built with `HtmlDocument::from_html` and a `DocumentConfig` with a `ColorScheme::Light` viewport and `HtmlProvider`, then `resolve(0.0)` (tests/blitz-tests/tests/accessibility_hidden.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/comment_layout.rs:); scenarios run in both layout modes `for incremental in [false, true]` (tests/blitz-tests/tests/anonymous_block_cache_invalidation.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/flex_grid_order.rs:); descriptive snake_case test names (tests/blitz-tests/tests/dir_attribute.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/dir_attribute.rs:); `#[track_caller]` assertion helpers (tests/blitz-tests/tests/accessibility_roles.rs:); some tests use `test_that` matchers returning `TestResult<()>` (tests/blitz-tests/tests/accessibility_hidden.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/accessibility_hidden.rs:); a pointer-event builder is repeated per file (tests/blitz-tests/tests/details_element.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/fragment_navigation.rs:;
 tests/blitz-tests/tests/scrollbar_drag.rs:), while the seven_guis stand checks state the tables and helpers they share once, in the module `tests/blitz-tests/tests/common/mod.rs`, which a check reads by declaring `mod common;` — a module in a subdirectory of the integration-test directory is no test target, so each test-target file still covers one behavior (tests/blitz-tests/tests/common/mod.rs:) — and the checks that hold a `Session` — the session checks, the settle check `stand_settle`, the sixteen driver-action checks `stand_act_*`, since 2026-10-10-mcp-surface `stand_mcp_tools`, since 2026-10-12-headless-screenshot, `stand_screenshot_repeat` and, since 2026-10-14-stand-requirement-sweep, the driver sweep `stand_requirement_sweep`, the served-page checks `stand_cells_served` and `stand_load_page` and `stand_graph`, which also reads `common`'s `GRAPH_DRIVES`, and the actions check `stand_actions`, which also reads `common`'s `ACTIONABLE_AT_BOOT` — keep what they share in a second such module, `tests/blitz-tests/tests/session_common/mod.rs`, read by `mod session_common;` (tests/blitz-tests/tests/session_common/mod.rs:); tests first assert the fixture produces the condition under test (tests/blitz-tests/tests/anonymous_block_leak.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/interaction_state_canonicalization.rs:); WPT test names use forward slashes relative to WPT_DIR (wpt/runner/src/main.rs:); the CI Python tests use `unittest` with one `TestCase` per function (.github/scripts/test_wpt_diff_to_pr.py:; .github/scripts/test_wpt_diff_to_pr.py:); blitz-tests keeps one test target per file directly under `tests/` — its `[package]` sets no `autotests` — and the one file there that is declared and never built is upstream's single-binary `tests/all.rs`, the manifest's one `[[test]]` table, held at `test = false`: the operator's decision of 2026-10-10, a standing two-line difference from upstream's manifest that `BlitzTestsTargetsTest` pins (tests/blitz-tests/Cargo.toml:; .github/scripts/test_blitz_tests_targets.py:)

- **Dioxus DOM bridge:** DOM events route to the vdom via the nearest `data-dioxus-id` attribute parsed as `usize` (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/dioxus_document.rs:; packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/dioxus_document.rs:); listener registration sets a `"<rust func>"` placeholder and `data-dioxus-id` (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/mutation_writer.rs:); `__webview_document` sets a sub-document and `data` on `<object>` a custom widget (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/mutation_writer.rs:); `style`-namespace attributes become style properties and `dangerous_inner_html` sets inner HTML (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/mutation_writer.rs:); a falsy value (Bool false, Text `"false"`, Int 0, Float 0.0, None) of one of the 28 names of upstream's private predicate `is_bool_attr`, a closed `matches!` set — `allowfullscreen`, `allowpaymentrequest`, `async`, `autofocus`, `autoplay`, `checked`, `controls`, `default`, `defer`, `disabled`, `formnovalidate`, `hidden`, `ismap`, `itemscope`, `loop`, `multiple`, `muted`, `nomodule`, `novalidate`, `open`, `playsinline`, `readonly`, `required`, `reversed`, `selected`, `truespeed`, `webkitdirectory` and `inert` — the 27 names before `inert` the module-private `BOOLEAN_ATTRIBUTES` list of escher's own, retired at 2026-10-13-upstream-sync-polish-and-ship for upstream's predicate (#1188), which left `mutation_writer.rs` byte-identical to upstream's at `434e0661` — clears the attribute, on the dynamic and the static template attribute path alike; a truthy value of a listed name is still written, and an attribute outside the list holding `false` (`aria-hidden`, a `data-` attribute) is still written as `"false"`; a framework-written `inert: false` is cleared and leaves content as it was, while a truthy `inert` makes its subtree inert
 — not focusable, not hit, out of the accessibility tree and the snapshot (§Established Decisions → DOM semantics); so an element rendered `hidden: false` carries no `hidden` attribute, is laid out and is a snapshot node, and one rendered with a truthy `hidden` is excluded (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/mutation_writer.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/dioxus_falsy_boolean_attrs.rs:) — as measured at escher-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-06-snapshot-state-fidelity/report.md and, for the 28-name predicate, as measured at escher-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-13-upstream-sync-polish-and-ship/report.md; `SubDocumentAttr` / `CustomWidgetAttr` write-once, compared by id (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/write_once_attr.rs:); event converter for form, mouse, keyboard, focus, mounted, pointer, scroll, touch, wheel (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/events.rs:); `NodeHandle` backs `MountedData` (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/events.rs:); `DioxusDocument` exposes `vdom`, `inner`, `vdom_state.try_element_to_node_id(ElementId)`, which the guard of `flush_queued_mounted_events` (below) also reads (tests/blitz-tests/tests/stale_node_mapping.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/stale_node_mapping.rs:); **stable element ids** — `DioxusDocument::element_id(NodeId) -> Option<String>` (`None` for a non-element, stale or detached node, never panics) and `element_ids() -> Vec<(NodeId, String)>` (every element reachable from the document root, document pre-order, ids pairwise distinct) compute the id on demand from a vdom walk joined with a DOM walk and write it to neither the DOM nor the vdom (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/dioxus_document.rs:; packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/element_id.rs:); the id is one of four tiers, in order of precedence — (1) the **author key** — the HTML `id` verbatim when non-empty, `/`-free and the first in document pre-order to claim it — else (2) the **anchored path**
 — for an element under an element of its own component instance that reads an author key: that key, `//`, then one element segment per DOM level below the keyed element (`box//p:0/b:0`), the nearest such keyed element anchoring — else (3) the **component path** — `/`-joined component segments below the app root, where dioxus-core's four framework scopes (`ScopeId::ROOT` · `ROOT_SUSPENSE_BOUNDARY` · `ROOT_ERROR_BOUNDARY` · `APP`, the user root dioxus-core names `"root"`) add none, a segment is the last `::` segment of `VComponent.name` (dioxus-core's full type path) with generics stripped, and an owner's 2nd and later instance of one name reads `{name}:{k}`, then one element segment per DOM level, `{tag}[{key}]` for a template root of a Dioxus-keyed VNode (non-empty, `/`-free, first among its siblings) else `{tag}:{n}` with `n` the index among same-parent, same-owner-instance, same-tag element siblings; a template root owned by its DOM parent's owner (a list row, an `if` branch) continues the parent's path — the parent's own path, or `{key}/` when the parent reads its author key, which is what spells the anchored tier's `//` — one owned by another component restarts at that component's chain whatever keyed element it sits under, so an anchor acts inside one component only (`div#slot { Item {} }` leaves Item's root at `Item/b:0`), and the app root's own elements read `/{tag}:{n}…` — else (4) the **document path** of an element no component renders, `/` then `{tag}:{n}` per DOM level from the document root (`/html:0/body:0`); an `id` that fails the author-key predicate (empty, `/`-bearing, a later duplicate) anchors nothing, and the skeleton's `main` reads its key yet anchors nothing in a Dioxus app, its children being another owner's template roots; no id of one tier equals an id of another
 — an author key holds no `/`, and an anchored path is the only id holding `//`, a component path being non-empty names and segments and a document or app-root path starting with one `/` with no empty segment after it — so a key spelled like a component name stays distinct (`Item//b:0` beside `Item/b:0`); beside the four tiers the grammar states a fifth, reserved form, `{owner}///{key}`, which no tier can hold and no caller produces, the walk not calling it — `VIRTUAL_KEY_SEPARATOR` (`"///"`) and `virtual_node_id(owner, key) -> Option<String>`, public and re-exported, spell it, answering `None` for an empty or `/`-bearing key (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/element_id.rs:); the anchored tier, its `key//segment` spelling and its one-component reach are ratified by the founder (2026-10-06) (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/element_id.rs:; packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/element_id.rs:; packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/element_id.rs:; packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/element_id.rs:); no `NodeId`, `ElementId`, `ScopeId` or pointer enters an id; the id **holds across code edits** on the stand, in both layout modes — every element of each lean task reads the same id under an edited shell (an earlier sibling of the task root's tag, a wrapper around the task, one more level), every element that reads an author key under a same-tag sibling added before it, a sibling removed and a wrapped ancestor, every anchored element under an edit outside its keyed element, and every element under an edit of text, an attribute, a class or a handler — while an earlier same-tag sibling or a wrapper inserted between an unkeyed element and the point its path starts at changes that element's id and no keyed element's (tests/blitz-tests/tests/stand_id_edits.rs:) — as measured at escher-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-06-id-stability-across-code-edits/report.md; on the stand the chrome reads its author keys (`main`, `task-shell`, `back-btn` …), the task root `TaskShell/Counter/div:0`, a CRUD row its author key `crud-person-0` and Home's seven cards `task-card-{slug}` (tests/blitz-tests/tests/stand_element_ids.rs:;
 tests/blitz-tests/tests/stand_actionable_keys.rs:), each card's five children reading anchored paths (`task-card-counter//div:1/h2:0`) and no lean-task element reading one at boot — as measured at escher-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-06-id-stability-across-code-edits/report.md; a node a re-render removes is dropped with its descendants by the bridge's crate-private `remove_and_drop_node` / `replace_and_drop_node`, their `element_id_mapping` / `node_id_mapping` entries cleared, so it no longer resolves and reads no id — `get_node` `None`, `element_id` `None`, no pair in `element_ids()` (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/mutation_writer.rs:; packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/element_id.rs:) — upstream #1185, as measured at escher-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-13-upstream-sync-polish-and-ship/report.md; until that chunk it was DETACHED (parent `None`) and kept resolving until its ElementId was reassigned, as measured at escher-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-06-stable-element-ids/report.md; the id **persists** on the stand, in both layout modes and on each lean task: every element present before and after a re-render reads the same id; a remount (the task's card → Back → the same card in `app::app`) reads the same id list in order, equal to `stand::boot`'s, on fresh `NodeId`s for every element except the four document-skeleton elements `DioxusDocument::new` builds outside the VirtualDom (`/html:0`, `/html:0/head:0`, `/html:0/body:0`, `main`), and every dropped pre-remount `NodeId` reads `None`; a second process reads the same id lists (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/dioxus_document.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/stand_id_persistence.rs:);
 the lean CRUD row's Dioxus key is its person's model-assigned `u64` (`key: "{person.id}"`, fixture people 0–2, Create from 3), set on the `for` item itself with the filter in the iterator, so the list diffs by key, and the row carries `id: "crud-person-{person.id}"` beside that key, so a row's id is its author key `crud-person-{person.id}` (fixture `crud-person-0..2`, Create from `crud-person-3`) and follows its person under filter, Create and Delete — a `key:` on an element inside an `if` within a `for` body does not key the list, whose items are then diffed by position (examples/seven_guis/src/tasks/crud.rs:) — as measured at escher-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-06-id-persistence/report.md; under the `accessibility` feature `DioxusDocument` overrides `Document::accessibility_tree`: it builds the base tree, maps `element_ids()` by `NodeId::as_u64()` and sets each element node's AccessKit `author_id` to that element's stable id, while `TextRun`s, the document root and the synthetic `Window` (`u64::MAX`) carry none and no tree id indexes the slab — so the id reaches the accessibility tree the harness reads and the platform tree the shell builds, and is written to neither the DOM nor the vdom (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/dioxus_document.rs:; tests/blitz-tests/tests/stand_accessibility_ids.rs:); dioxus-native-dom's `accessibility` feature reads `["blitz-dom/accessibility", "dep:accesskit"]` and dioxus-native's `accessibility` forwards to it, so a crate taking either Dioxus crate with `default-features = false` (the workspace pin) gets the override, the snapshot model, its text form, its diff and the actionable-key check only by naming the feature or by depending on a crate that names it — blitz-tests and, since 2026-10-07-act-by-id, escher-driver name it on dioxus-native-dom;
 seven_guis names none in its own manifest and gets it through its `escher-driver` edge, so both its binaries compile these modules, with no platform adapter (packages/dioxus-native-dom/Cargo.toml:; packages/dioxus-native/Cargo.toml:; tests/blitz-tests/Cargo.toml:; packages/escher-driver/Cargo.toml:) — as measured at escher-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-06-accessibility-tree-identity/report.md; under the same feature `DioxusDocument::snapshot(&self) -> Snapshot` reads the screen as a tree, read-only: it builds from that `accessibility_tree` override and walks its `children` lists from `TreeInfo.root`; `Snapshot { roots: Vec<SnapshotNode> }` offers `nodes()` (pre-order) and `get(&str)` (by stable element id), a `SnapshotNode` carries `id: String`, `role: accesskit::Role`, `name: String`, `state: NodeState`, `bounds: blitz_dom::BoundingRect` and `children`, and `NodeState` carries eight fields in the order `enabled: Option<bool>` · `checked: Option<bool>` · `value: Option<String>` · `selected: Option<bool>` · `pressed: Option<bool>` · `invalid: bool` · `focused: bool` · `clickable: bool` — four until 2026-10-10-snapshot-states added the three after `value`, with no new public type, so a struct literal without a spread names all eight (packages/dioxus-native-dom/src/snapshot.rs:) — all three `Debug`, `Clone` and `PartialEq`, re-exported from the crate, together with the public constant `MASKED_VALUE: &str` (eight U+2022 characters), and through dioxus-native's `pub use dioxus_native_dom::*`; a node is a tree node carrying an `author_id` whose element resolves through `BaseDocument::get_node` — the `Window`, the document root and `TextRun`s are not nodes, their kept descendants attach to the nearest kept ancestor, generic containers are kept, and what the accessibility tree excludes never appears; every field is an existing reader's
